.Dd November 2, 2026
.Dt AKTURE 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm akture
.Nd X11 display manager
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl d
.Op Fl f Ar config
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
starts the X server, shows a login screen and starts the session of
the user who logged in.
When the session ends, the X server is restarted and the login screen
is shown again.
.Pp
.Nm
is split in two processes.
The master, which runs as root, starts the X server, authenticates
users with
.Xr pam 3 ,
runs hooks and sessions, and shuts the machine down on request.
Everything that handles input from outside \(em drawing, keyboard,
background pictures and fonts \(em is done by
.Xr akture-greeter 8 ,
which runs as the unprivileged user
.Dq _akture
and talks to the master only over a socketpair with a fixed set of
fixed-size messages.
Any malformed or unexpected message gets the greeter killed and
restarted.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl d
Do not detach, and log to the standard error as well.
.It Fl f Ar config
Read
.Ar config
instead of
.Pa /etc/akture/akture.conf .
.El
.Ss Authentication
Passwords are never checked by
.Nm
itself.
The PAM service
.Dq akture
is used for normal logins and
.Dq akture-autologin
for the user named by
.Cm autologin_user ,
which is logged in once at boot.
From the third consecutive failure on, the answer is delayed by two
more seconds each time, up to 30 seconds.
Unknown users and wrong passwords give the same answer.
.Ss Sessions
Sessions are described by files in
.Pa /etc/akture/sessions ,
in name order, each with a
.Cm name
and an
.Cm exec
line; see
.Xr akture.conf 5 .
The session child applies the login class with
.Xr setusercontext 3 ,
then calls
.Xr setgroups 2 ,
.Xr setgid 2
and
.Xr setuid 2
in this order and exits at the first failure without running anything.
It writes the X authority to
.Pa ~/.Xauthority
as the user, and records the login in
.Xr utmpx 5 .
A session that ends within ten seconds is reported as failed on the
login screen.
.Ss Hooks
At the events below, the executable files in
.Pa /etc/akture/hooks.d
are run in name order with the event and its arguments, without a
shell:
.Bl -tag -width "session-started" -offset indent
.It Cm greeter-shown
the last user, or an empty string
.It Cm auth-ok
user and session
.It Cm session-started
user and session
.It Cm session-ended
user
.El
.Pp
Hooks run as root, one after the other, in a separate process that
.Nm
does not wait for; each is killed after ten seconds.
Only files owned by root and writable by nobody else, in a directory
with the same property, are run.
Failures are logged and otherwise ignored.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/var/db/akture/last-user" -compact
.It Pa /etc/akture/akture.conf
configuration
.It Pa /etc/akture/sessions/
session definitions
.It Pa /etc/akture/hooks.d/
hooks
.It Pa /etc/pam.d/akture
PAM configuration
.It Pa /var/db/akture/last-user
the last user to log in, readable by root only
.It Pa /var/run/akture/
X authority files of the server and the greeter
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits 0 when terminated by a signal and 1 when it gives up, e.g.\&
because the X server does not start or the greeter keeps failing;
the console is then left to
.Xr getty 8 .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr akture-conv 1 ,
.Xr pam 3 ,
.Xr setusercontext 3 ,
.Xr akture.conf 5 ,
.Xr akture-greeter 8
.Sh SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
Passwords and answers to PAM are erased from memory once used and are
never logged.
The name in a failed attempt is not logged either, because it may be
a password typed into the wrong field.
